REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"w04","title":"Writing to argue, instruct and influence; reflective essays","summary":"How to write when people disagree (argue), when every step must be exact (a lab manual) and when you want to move a reader (influence), and how to plan, write and revise a reflective essay in 30 minutes. Each kind of writing has its own words and its own structure.","n":4,"lectures":[{"code":"4.1","title":"Writing to argue - I | strategies, style & structure for persuasive writing","video":"le0gtMHfD0A","seconds":1289},{"code":"4.2","title":"Writing to argue - II | techniques, structure & examples for strong arguments","video":"-Aunqsk8w0o","seconds":1095},{"code":"4.3","title":"Lab manual | writing clear, precise & effective procedural instructions","video":"F0CnZHLSyYs","seconds":1310},{"code":"4.4","title":"Writing to influence | persuasive techniques, ethics & critical analysis","video":"fd70FMSa9rg","seconds":1121},{"code":"4.5","title":"Essays on reflection- I | planning, structuring & expressing complex ideas","video":"cE0ZsSI9UKk","seconds":1568},{"code":"4.6","title":"Essays on reflection- II | structure, cohesion & tips for exam success","video":"5QlR0QUXeAQ","seconds":1445}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"writing-to-argue-content-and-language","level":2,"title":"Writing to argue: content and language"},{"id":"where-the-content-comes-from","level":3,"title":"Where the content comes from"},{"id":"two-sides-or-more","level":3,"title":"Two sides, or more"},{"id":"the-language-of-argument","level":3,"title":"The language of argument"},{"id":"finding-argument-words-in-a-report","level":3,"title":"Finding argument words in a report"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-essay-children-computers-and-cellphones","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s essay: children, computers and cellphones"},{"id":"the-structure-of-an-argument","level":2,"title":"The structure of an argument"},{"id":"where-to-put-your-main-point","level":3,"title":"Where to put your main point"},{"id":"the-usual-four-part-structure","level":3,"title":"The usual four-part structure"},{"id":"evidence-statistics-or-anecdotes","level":3,"title":"Evidence: statistics or anecdotes"},{"id":"sentences-of-argument","level":3,"title":"Sentences of argument"},{"id":"two-extracts-on-working-parents","level":3,"title":"Two extracts on working parents"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-model-essay-big-chain-stores","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s model essay: big chain stores"},{"id":"writing-a-lab-manual","level":2,"title":"Writing a lab manual"},{"id":"four-kinds-of-exact-words","level":3,"title":"Four kinds of exact words"},{"id":"why-experts-make-mistakes","level":3,"title":"Why experts make mistakes"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-manual-making-tea","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s manual: making tea"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-model-preparing-coffee","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s model: preparing coffee"},{"id":"writing-to-influence","level":2,"title":"Writing to influence"},{"id":"is-it-right-to-influence","level":3,"title":"Is it right to influence?"},{"id":"words-that-hide-quantity","level":3,"title":"Words that hide quantity"},{"id":"percentages-without-people-the-surveys","level":3,"title":"Percentages without people: the surveys"},{"id":"the-practice-report-and-essay","level":3,"title":"The practice report and essay"},{"id":"essays-on-reflection-planning-with-a-synopsis","level":2,"title":"Essays on reflection: planning with a synopsis"},{"id":"do-not-start-writing-at-once","level":3,"title":"Do not start writing at once"},{"id":"what-a-synopsis-is","level":3,"title":"What a synopsis is"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-synopsis-on-happiness","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s synopsis on happiness"},{"id":"why-the-five-minutes-are-worth-it","level":3,"title":"Why the five minutes are worth it"},{"id":"a-partisan-synopsis-and-a-better-one","level":3,"title":"A partisan synopsis, and a better one"},{"id":"essays-on-reflection-time-stories-cohesion-and-drafts","level":2,"title":"Essays on reflection: time, stories, cohesion and drafts"},{"id":"plan-your-30-minutes","level":3,"title":"Plan your 30 minutes"},{"id":"stories-make-your-essay-different","level":3,"title":"Stories make your essay different"},{"id":"cohesion-the-glue-of-an-essay","level":3,"title":"Cohesion: the glue of an essay"},{"id":"drafts-the-happiness-essay","level":3,"title":"Drafts: the happiness essay"},{"id":"cutting-to-the-word-limit","level":3,"title":"Cutting to the word limit"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Writing to argue</strong> (4.1): policies are rarely undisputed, so a good argument presents two sides, or more. Its language is special: <em>on one hand … on the other, as far as, in my view, on considering</em>; comparison words (<em>the <strong>least</strong> polluting option</em>); adverbs (<em>actually</em>); nouns for groups (<em>majority, half the population</em>); and expressions demanding consideration (<em>before you conclude, in totality</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>The structure of an argument</strong> (4.2): the situation first, then arguments for one side, then (in the third or fourth paragraph) arguments for the other side, and finally an overview with an acceptable conclusion. Use statistics for policy questions and anecdotes among friends and family.</li>\n<li><strong>Sentences of argument</strong> (4.2): complex sentences built on <em>If … then</em>, <em>While …</em>, <em>Whereas …</em>, <em>As … so</em>. Even two simple sentences argue when they contrast: <em>Stars twinkle, planets do not.</em></li>\n<li><strong>A lab manual</strong> (4.3) is written like a recipe. It needs exact words for things and actions (nouns and verbs), their sequence, their duration and the changes that show progress. Experts go wrong by skipping “obvious” steps.</li>\n<li><strong>Writing to influence</strong> (4.4) leans on vague quantity words (<em>numerous, several, recent weeks, a strong preference, continuing to rise</em>) and on percentages without the number of people behind them. Always ask: <strong>“of how many?”</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Reflective essays</strong> (4.5) are on topics with no black-or-white answer. Do not start writing at once: first make a <strong>synopsis</strong>, an overview “from the rooftop” that covers <strong>all</strong> aspects of the topic, not just one side.</li>\n<li><strong>The 30-minute plan</strong> (4.6): 5 minutes for the synopsis, 20 for writing, 5 for revising. Give about 20–25% of the words to stories and instances, join sentences with cohesive devices (<em>however, therefore, that is why</em>, pronouns, prepositions), and expect to write more than one draft.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"writing-to-argue-content-and-language\">Writing to argue: content and language</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The English 1 writing lessons covered the basics: correct language and simple content. This module goes higher: <strong>style</strong>, <strong>appropriateness</strong> (the right words for the purpose and the reader), <strong>discourse features</strong> and richer content <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>. Its first purpose is <strong>arguing</strong>: writing about a question on which people disagree. The lecture calls this session “online grocery”: should big companies sell our daily groceries, or should this trade be left to local shops? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The debate, as the lecture sets it out <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Leave it to local shops</th>\n<th>Let big companies in</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Local shops employ hundreds of thousands of people.</td>\n<td>Nobody loses a job; only the kind of job changes. Shops may even sell more.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Groceries are part of culture: the coffee you like may come only from Kumbakonam. Such things may disappear in a global market.</td>\n<td>Nothing of the culture is lost: “Idli would still be Idli; coffee would still be coffee.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Lower cost and more choice (coffee from Kumbakonam or from Puttur; idli from Murugan Idli or from Woodlands), delivered to your door. You can use the money and time you save elsewhere.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“There are arguments both sides, and deciding is not easy.” So the lecture asks: <strong>how do you write when you argue?</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"where-the-content-comes-from\">Where the content comes from</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Content</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Content</strong> is what you have to say: the ideas, facts and examples. It is “the mainstay” (the main support) of any writing or speech: “unless you have things to say”, language has nothing to carry. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>How to build content for a topic such as “Should big companies be allowed into the retail grocery trade?” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Note what you already know</strong>, as points: a synopsis, an overview of what you will write. (Synopses come back in Lecture 4.5.)</li>\n<li><strong>Look at local examples.</strong> The lecture’s example: an old woman who brought a small basket of guavas every morning, sold them all by afternoon, and fed her family from the sales. Is she still there? If yes, she shows that local sellers can survive big companies. If not, she shows how threatened such sellers are. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Search</strong> the internet, books and other sources for statistics, examples and <strong>anecdotes</strong> (short true stories). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Think about related cases.</strong> Big companies already sell clothes, meat, fish and milk; what is so special about grocery? If milk selling could be centralised in huge countries like the USA, China or the USSR, why not in a small town? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"two-sides-or-more\">Two sides, or more</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Just as we speak, we often write to argue.</li>\n<li>Policies are rarely undisputed.</li>\n<li>There always are two sides, or even more.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Writing to argue</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Writing to argue</strong> presents <strong>two or more sides</strong> of a disputed question, with reasons and evidence for each side. The question is usually about a <strong>policy</strong>: a plan of action of a public body, such as a city corporation, a state government or the central government. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>Policy debates are everywhere: TV debates, newspaper forums, radio talks, social media.</li>\n<li>A policy is “seldom, nearly never” <strong>undisputed</strong>, <strong>unanimous</strong> (agreed by everyone) or <strong>universal</strong>. In the end someone has to decide “in the larger interest”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a></li>\n<li>Before deciding, that manager or leader reads documents, and these documents “ideally should present both sides or all sides”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture’s model of a live argument is a TED debate, “Does the world need nuclear energy?”: can nuclear energy replace carbon fuels? The slide asks you to watch its first 14 minutes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UK8ccWSZkic\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the video</a>) and to <strong>mark the language</strong>, not only the content: “it is the language, an appropriate language which conveys your preference for one kind of opinion … that will win your audience.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=644s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:44</a></p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Speaking and writing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Writing uses spelling and visible words; speech uses sound and audible words. But in both, “arguments are presented in a very special kind of language”. So we learn two things: how to collect and present content, and the language for presenting it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-language-of-argument\">The language of argument</h3>\n<p>The slide: “Language of argument is different. Here we need to talk at least about two competing issues. Language, therefore should be appropriate.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=774s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:54</a> The lecture shows five kinds of words:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Expressions that weigh sides and views</strong>: <em>on one hand … the other, as far as, as well as, point of view, in view, in my view, in view of, on considering</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=774s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:54</a></li>\n<li><strong>Adjectives of comparison</strong>, which rank one option above another: “solar power is the <strong>least</strong> polluting option”; “it is the <strong>most</strong> easily available option”. Also <em>most, more, less, little, much, many</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=792s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:12</a></li>\n<li><strong>Adverbs</strong>, which press the writer’s preference: “Mass rapid transport systems are not only the <strong>most</strong> cost effective options, but <strong>actually</strong> that is the only future of big cities.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=837s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:57</a></li>\n<li><strong>Nouns for groups</strong> (not proper nouns), which show “groups and sections of opinion, their size, their power”: “<strong>Majority</strong> groups have always been ignored unless they have assured profit.” “<strong>Half the population</strong> in metro cities never works…” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Expressions demanding consideration</strong>, which ask for the reader’s attention: <em>before you conclude, while we are at it, one can as well see, in totality</em> (“in totality, not in partiality”). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=945s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:45</a></li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Taking apart an argument sentence (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=837s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:57</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Mass rapid transport systems are not only the most cost effective options, but actually that is the only future of big cities.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>cost effective</em> is an adjective; it describes the noun <em>options</em>.</li>\n<li><em>most</em> raises it to the top degree of comparison. (<em>least cost effective</em> would push the other way.)</li>\n<li><em>actually</em> is an adverb. It adds the writer’s pressure: this is not only good, it is a fact.</li>\n<li><em>the only future</em> closes every other door. In the lecturer’s words, the writer “wants us to believe that there are no options”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>most</em> (comparison), <em>actually</em> (adverb) and <em>the only</em> carry the argument. Without them the sentence is a plain statement: <em>Mass rapid transport systems are cost effective options for big cities.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Half the population”: half of whom?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Group words sound big, but they hide the count. The lecturer’s example: an advertisement says “half the people we surveyed said ours is the best soap”. How many did they survey? Perhaps 500 people, in a country of 1,000 million: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">500</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.0000005</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> that is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.00005%</span></span></span></span>,</span> far less than even 1% of the people. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Make your own glossary</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s advice: read about some currently debated subjects that interest you, and “make a glossary of such terms” (a <strong>glossary</strong> is your own list of words with their meanings and uses). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a> <em>Actually</em> was also a discourse marker that adds emphasis in <a href=\"#/english2/w01/discourse-markers\">Week 1</a>, and phrases like <em>in my opinion</em> were comment adverbs in <a href=\"#/english2/w02/comment-viewpoint-and-focus-adverbs\">Week 2</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"finding-argument-words-in-a-report\">Finding argument words in a report</h3>\n<p>The lecture took paragraphs from a long report on online grocery. The highlighted words are there because the writer is arguing that online grocery is growing <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=1011s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:51</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It’s <strong>no longer</strong> clothes or books or makeup items that people want to buy online. Even when it comes to buying the everyday groceries, people are <strong>increasingly</strong> turning online, amid the pandemic. A recent study indicates that online grocery shopping is <strong>likely</strong> to grow more than two-fold as more shoppers are now ordering items online.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture’s Activity 1 then asks you to find similar words in two more paragraphs <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=1049s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:29</a>. The bold words are the lecture’s model answers <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=1099s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:19</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Amazon and Flipkart have also seen an <strong>increase</strong> in grocery orders. As reported in Economic Times, BigBasket says that since March, its new customer acquisition grew <strong>three-fold</strong> in terms of orders, <strong>compared</strong> to pre-COVID, while retention of new customer cohorts has <strong>increased</strong> by 60%.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Grocery is the largest consumer segment <strong>by far</strong> in the Indian market, and Indians spend <strong>more than 50%</strong> of their monthly income on groceries. In addition, the retail market in India has undergone a <strong>major</strong> transformation and has witnessed tremendous <strong>growth</strong> in the past 10 years. The <strong>overall</strong> retail market is set to cross the $1.75 trillion mark by 2026, according to data available with Invest India – during which period India’s e-commerce market is set to <strong>grow</strong> at a CAGR of 30% for gross merchandise value to be worth $200 billion <strong>by</strong> 2026.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Some business words: <strong>customer acquisition</strong> is winning new customers; <strong>retention</strong> is keeping them; a <strong>cohort</strong> is a group that started at the same time; <strong>CAGR</strong> (compound annual growth rate) is the average growth per year; <strong>gross merchandise value</strong> is the total value of goods sold.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What the words do (my grouping)</th>\n<th>Words from the three paragraphs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>show change over time</td>\n<td><em>no longer, increasingly, increase, increased, growth, grow</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>compare and size up</td>\n<td><em>three-fold, compared, by far, more than 50%, major, overall</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>predict</td>\n<td><em>likely</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>set a time limit</td>\n<td><em>by</em> (2026)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-essay-children-computers-and-cellphones\">The lecture’s essay: children, computers and cellphones</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s Activity 2 <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=1137s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:57</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In about 200 words write an essay on the following topic: Some people say young children should not have unrestricted access to computers and cellphones, they can harm themselves. Some others say that everyone has to learn to use these things, so why not start early. Examine the two sides of this issue and present your own opinion on this subject. Use your own ideas, arguments and evidence to support your arguments and conclusions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture’s synopsis <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=1224s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:24</a>, points joined by dashes:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>We live in computer age – but computer is not ours – difficulties in using computers – demanding in nature – instruction required – important for job and life – harm from computers to mental and physical health – addiction – detrimental to social skills – better begin early – create safeguards – parent lock.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The model answer <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=1248s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:48</a>, exactly 200 words:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The twenty-first century may be inseparable from the existence of computers and cellphones, but children having unrestricted access to such devices is a contested notion.</p>\n<p>Access to computers and cellphones implies access to various communication platforms, digital, mass and social media and the internet. Children having unrestricted access to this endless source of information is the digital equivalent of leaving a child alone in a crowded amusement park. Lack of guidance and supervision can harm the child.</p>\n<p>However, computer skills are essential in both personal and professional settings. In today’s setting, mobile devices and digital media are part of a child’s surrounding growing up. A child’s environment determines the growth and aptitude of a child. By restricting access to everyday objects and not letting children experiment, adults may dampen the natural curiosity of a child and make way for the child to act out.</p>\n<p>Although it may not be ideal or possible to keep children away from computers, it can be argued that restricted access in the form of child locks, filters, adult supervision, guidance and limited screen-time are necessary. With the internet being unrestricted, it is upon adults to oversee usage to ensure a healthy growing environment for children.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The synopsis points reappear in the essay: <em>harm</em> in paragraph 2 (a “contested notion” is an idea people argue about; the amusement-park comparison makes the danger easy to picture), <em>important for job and life</em> and <em>better begin early</em> in paragraph 3, and <em>create safeguards, parent lock</em> in paragraph 4 (“child locks, filters, adult supervision”). Its four paragraphs follow the structure that Lecture 4.2 names; the table in that section takes them apart.</p>\n<p>Another topic on the last slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=1280s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:20</a>: “Should one of the parents stay at home until the child is at least eight years old? Support your answer with ideas, arguments and evidences.” Lecture 4.2 starts from this question.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Writing is a skill</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Skills are best learned by doing again and again”, until you reach perfection. Write your own essay first, then compare it with the model; if you are not happy with it, write it again. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"the-structure-of-an-argument\">The structure of an argument</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 4.2 is called “Home alone”: should one parent stay at home with the child? It looks at two things: how an argument is <strong>structured</strong> (what comes first, next and last), and what kinds of <strong>sentences</strong> carry it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"where-to-put-your-main-point\">Where to put your main point</h3>\n<p>“Do you bring your best point in the beginning?” The lecturer shows three choices on the parents question <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>. (He adds: “Sorry, these are not my personal views.” They illustrate structure, not facts.)</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>The lecturer’s illustration</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>At the beginning</td>\n<td>“I am for mothers staying at home. It may be boring, it may be uneconomical, but this is the best way, time tested way to raise a kid.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At the end</td>\n<td>“So finally, I am sure no one has any doubts that one parent must stay home and this must be the mother.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In the middle, after the evidence</td>\n<td>First the evidence: at least 17 percent of fathers stayed home, but spent the time on their phones and computer games, <em>whereas</em> the mothers “paid the best attention to the child”. Then the point: so mothers should stay home.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“A structure has to be there.” Before you start, decide what you will say first, what next and what last. All the choices are yours, with one limit: keep your reader’s interest. “Are the listeners bored? … Have they already begun praying that our presentation ended?” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">How to keep the reader with you</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Keep it interesting, short and sweet. Illustrate it with examples. Occasionally bring some statistical evidence”, and make a valid point without being biased to “either this side or that side”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-usual-four-part-structure\">The usual four-part structure</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Structure of an argument essay</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=106s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:46</a>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Usually, a <strong>statement of the situation</strong> is given first,</li>\n<li>followed by <strong>arguments for one side</strong> against the other.</li>\n<li>In the third or fourth paragraph come <strong>arguments in favour of the other side</strong>, against the one stated earlier.</li>\n<li>The <strong>final part</strong> usually takes an <strong>overview</strong> and reaches an <strong>acceptable conclusion</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>Both of the lecture’s model essays follow this exactly (see the table after the second model essay below).</p>\n<h3 id=\"evidence-statistics-or-anecdotes\">Evidence: statistics or anecdotes</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Statistical and anecdotal evidence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=506s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:26</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Statistical evidence</strong> (numbers from surveys and studies) is common “at larger policy levels”: when you write for a state or a big organisation.</li>\n<li><strong>Anecdotal evidence</strong> (short true stories from experience) is common “in social circles among friends and family”.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Each kind can appear at the other level too. Build your structure with “a judicious mix” (a sensible mix), “like salt in sambar”: not too much of either. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"sentences-of-argument\">Sentences of argument</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Complex sentence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>complex sentence</strong> has one main (independent) clause and one or more subordinate clauses (<a href=\"#/english2/w03/complex-sentences-and-the-kinds-of-clause\">Week 3</a>). Arguments often use two-clause and three-clause sentences, because one clause can state a condition or a contrast and the other the claim. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer’s spoken examples <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Either parent can stay home, but the best care has been given by mothers.”</li>\n<li>“<strong>While</strong> there have been lots of studies saying that fathers are not the best caregivers, there, however, are nearly 13 percent fathers who gave better care to the child than perhaps any mother could.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“Look at the syntax: while, whereas, if, then.” <strong>Syntax</strong> is the way words are put together into sentences. “You should have a very good grip on syntax, so that you present arguments concisely, precisely, and effectively.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></p>\n<p>The four patterns on the slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=571s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:31</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>If …, then …</em></td>\n<td><em>If big stores deliver for less, then small shops must find new ways to compete.</em> (my example)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>While …, this …</em></td>\n<td><em>While big stores cut prices, small shops know their customers by name.</em> (my example)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Whereas …, there …</em></td>\n<td><em>Whereas the fathers spent the time on their phones, the mothers paid full attention to the child.</em> (from the lecturer’s spoken example)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>As …, so …</em></td>\n<td><em>As you sow, so you reap.</em> (the lecture’s “famous adage”)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Stars twinkle, planets do not” (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Jawaharlal Nehru wrote letters to his daughter, when she was very young, to introduce her to the universe. One line from them:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Stars twinkle</em>: a simple clause, with subject <em>stars</em> and verb <em>twinkle</em>.</li>\n<li><em>planets do not</em>: another simple clause; <em>twinkle</em> is understood after <em>do not</em>.</li>\n<li>There is no <em>but</em> or <em>whereas</em>. Yet, placed side by side, the two clauses contrast, and the contrast makes the point.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> even simple sentences argue when they “occur in contrast”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Which kind of sentence?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer says that together the two simple clauses “make a complex sentence”. In the terms of <a href=\"#/english2/w03/complex-sentences-and-the-kinds-of-clause\">Week 3</a>, two independent clauses joined by a comma make a <strong>compound</strong> sentence, because neither depends on the other. The same is true of “Either parent can stay home, but the best care has been given by mothers”, which the lecturer gives as a complex sentence: two independent clauses joined by <em>but</em> make a compound sentence. The <em>while</em>-sentence above is truly complex. The lecture’s point is the contrast, not the label.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"two-extracts-on-working-parents\">Two extracts on working parents</h3>\n<p>The first extract, with the bold words of the slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=604s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:04</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>If</strong> this seems contradictory (why would a parent in a two-income household believe their life choices were damaging to their children?), <strong>(then)</strong> keep in mind that the past few decades have seen a sea change in working parenthood. In 1970, <strong>only</strong> about a third of households — 31 percent — had two parents working. <strong>At that time</strong>, the most common arrangement <strong>by far</strong> was that the father worked full-time <strong>while</strong> the mother stayed home. <strong>So</strong> it’s possible (okay, likely) <strong>that</strong> attitudes haven’t caught up with the times.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>What the lecture points out <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>If … (then):</strong> “then is not written there, but every sentence that begins with an if implies the presence of then”. In the same way, <em>you</em> is understood in an <strong>imperative</strong> (an order or a request): “Open the window, please” means “(You) open the window, please”.</li>\n<li><strong>sea change:</strong> an <strong>idiom</strong> (a fixed phrase with a special meaning) for a very big change. What percentage? No number is given.</li>\n<li><strong>only about a third, 31 percent:</strong> words about numbers instead of numbers. “31 percent of how many? Only about a third of households. A third of how many?”</li>\n<li><strong>by far, while, so … that:</strong> the contrast and result words that build the argument. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The second extract, again with the slide’s bold words <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=755s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:35</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Let’s <strong>set aside</strong> the fact that not every family consists of a mother and a father today, and <strong>let’s even</strong> set aside the fact that plenty of children are raised by one parent, not two. The data <strong>as presented here</strong> — that most parents work but continue to hold forth an ideal that the mom should stay home because it’s best for the children — <strong>completely ignores</strong> reality. <strong>For many families</strong>, it’s <strong>just not</strong> economically feasible for one parent to stay home full-time with children. …</p>\n<p>And, <strong>moreover</strong>, what exactly are we basing our concept of “best for the child” on, anyway? … What we do know is that going to preschool is very good for children, and this means spending some time, <strong>at least</strong>, away from home.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The story test</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“These are those words, which are there only because this document presents an argument. If this document were telling a story: ‘there was a king who had three daughters’, then these expressions will not be there.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=755s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:35</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Bold words</th>\n<th>Their job in the argument (my notes)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>set aside, let’s even set aside</em></td>\n<td>move some facts out of the way, to focus on the writer’s point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>as presented here</em></td>\n<td>keeps a distance from the other side’s data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>completely ignores, just not</em></td>\n<td>strong words that reject the other view</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>for many families</em></td>\n<td>a group noun without a number (how many?)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>moreover</em></td>\n<td>adds one more argument</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>at least</em></td>\n<td>sets a minimum the reader can easily accept</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-model-essay-big-chain-stores\">The lecture’s model essay: big chain stores</h3>\n<p>The activity <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=777s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:57</a>: write, in no more than 200 words, on one of these topics.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Should big chain stores be allowed into selling domestic items on global levels?</strong> Should “big global giants, multinationals” be banned from selling vegetables, fruits, milk, meat and bread? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>Must a child be taught in English from the time of birth?</strong> Some say yes: children learn a language best, and English is “nearly inevitable” for a global audience and a good job. Others say no: it robs children of their mother tongue and of “an entire culture” they could inherit “for no special and no extra effort”; a second language can be learned a little later. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Should the state have power to control our private lives?</strong> Some say what we do at home also affects public life, so the state should have this power. Others say “a state’s power ends in public domain”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p>“Examine the issues, present arguments and ideas and examples in support of both and then present your conclusion.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a> The model answer for topic 1 <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>, 200 words:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Globalisation has led to a revolution in the way trade is carried out. Whether such sea change is welcome in sectors such as domestic retail is often in question.</p>\n<p>Big chain stores being permitted to sell domestic items on a global level taps the potential of local markets and connects them worldwide. Every link in the global market stands to gain from this - domestic producers have a bigger market, vendors have a diverse consumer base, consumers get a variety of goods to choose from, local markets get linked to the global market and nation-states improve diplomatic bonds through the exchange of culture and goods.</p>\n<p>While everyone that is part of the global market may benefit from this exchange, those outside of it may stand to lose much from domestic items being sold by big chain stores. Competitive global market rate may be less than domestic rate, causing local vendors to lose their consumer base. Unable to keep up with the pace of large retail chains, traditional economies risk downfall.</p>\n<p>However, permitting global chain stores into domestic retail is the way to go as long as the risks are averted through sustained effort to integrate traditional economies with the global market.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The four parts in both model essays</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>Children and screens (4.1)</th>\n<th>Big chain stores (4.2)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Situation</td>\n<td>screens are part of life, <em>but</em> access for children is “a contested notion”</td>\n<td>globalisation changed trade; is “such sea change” welcome?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. One side</td>\n<td>unrestricted access can harm the child</td>\n<td>every link gains: producers, vendors, consumers, nations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Other side</td>\n<td><em><strong>However</strong></em>, computer skills are essential</td>\n<td><em><strong>While</strong></em> insiders benefit, outsiders “may stand to lose much”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Conclusion</td>\n<td><em><strong>Although</strong></em> …, restricted access is necessary</td>\n<td><em><strong>However</strong></em>, let them in “as long as the risks are averted”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> both have four paragraphs in the same order, and each turn is marked by a contrast word (<em>However, While, Although</em>). Both conclusions are balanced: they accept the other side’s main point and add a condition.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Draft, compare, redraft</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Not one draft, not two drafts, do as many drafts as you can”, until you feel it can improve no further; then share it with friends, reviewers and teachers. In this “write and rewrite”, “not only your worldview, not only your language, but your total makeup as an intellectual grows”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"writing-a-lab-manual\">Writing a lab manual</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Students in the sciences, social sciences, life sciences, management and journalism often follow <strong>manuals</strong> for routine work and then write <strong>reports</strong> on it <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>. The lecture uses the lab that most of us know, the kitchen (“perhaps … the most useful laboratory … that human beings made for themselves”), and the simplest task in it: <strong>making tea</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Lab manual and lab report</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=56s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:56</a>: “Many students work in laboratories, write and use manuals of what they do, and later write reports on what they do and see, what they observe. Manuals are generally in the nature of recipes we often come across in learning to cook.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>lab manual</strong> tells a reader, step by step, how to do a task, like a <strong>recipe</strong> (written cooking instructions).</li>\n<li>A <strong>lab report</strong> tells, after the work, what was done and what was observed. Telling your family “today we cooked Dhokla”, with the ingredients and how you did it, is a small lab report. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Every region has its own recipes: coffee, Kerala-style payasam, Telangana-style rasam, fish as in North Bihar, Assam and Nepal, meat as in Kashmir, dhokla in Gujarat. Learning any of them means reading a manual. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"four-kinds-of-exact-words\">Four kinds of exact words</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">What a manual needs</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=252s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:12</a>: &quot;From the point of view of language, this is not a very complex piece of writing, but it is a very interesting one, and it demands greatest possible accuracy in terms of words for</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a. things and actions, i.e. nouns and verbs, etc.,</li>\n<li>b. sequence of their use,</li>\n<li>c. duration of their use,</li>\n<li>d. changes that may occur, etc.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer’s recap takes things and actions one at a time <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Things</strong> (nouns): the names of tools (vessels, pots, pans, utensils, spoons, ladles, forks) and of ingredients. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>Actions</strong> (verbs): “cooking is a big box” that holds many verbs: <em>boil, steam, simmer, fry, deep fry, stir fry, bake, roast, scald</em>. They are not the same: “Roast means one thing, bake means another, boil means one thing, steam means another.” Many dishes can be made in only one way, or only with two or three of these actions in a certain order. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>Sequence</strong> (order): “If you fry something before it is boiled, it will be wasted.” The lecture’s steamed potato: first steam it (hard boil it, do not soft boil it), then peel it, then fry it in a little oil. A standard idli needs no oil; a standard vada needs oil. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>Duration</strong> (how long): <em>until steam rises, until it boils, until it is lukewarm, until it is boiling hot</em>; in a restaurant, <em>well done</em> or <em>mild</em>. The lecturer’s rough times: hard-boiled “maybe for two minutes”, full boiled “perhaps four minutes”; in a microwave oven, 30 seconds can be enough. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Changes</strong> (how do we know it is done?): what we cook changes in form, smell, colour and taste. In many cultures, tasting before serving is taboo, so cooks judge by colour, form and smell. Some cooks can tell from the smell alone that the sambar has <em>uppu illai</em> (Tamil for “no salt”). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The same holds for any chemical or physical process. The lecturer adds that chemical changes happen “perhaps sooner, faster”, and physical changes over a longer span of time; take this as a rough remark, since rusting is a slow chemical change and melting ice a quick physical one. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"why-experts-make-mistakes\">Why experts make mistakes</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=787s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:07</a>: “It is possible that we know words for these things, but either through inattention, distraction, oversight, or sometimes even confusion about what to write first and what next, etc.”</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Cut it, fry it”: an expert’s manual (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An expert telling you how to fry potatoes says: “cut it, fry it, enough”. The real process, as the lecturer gives it:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Wash the potato.</li>\n<li>Wipe it with a clean towel.</li>\n<li>Keep it on the cutting board.</li>\n<li>Hold it between your thumb and index finger (unless you have a cutting machine).</li>\n<li>With a knife or a kitchen cutter, make equal-sized slices.</li>\n<li>Put oil in the pan first; add some spices if you like seasoning.</li>\n<li>Do not let it burn; do not let smoke arise.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> two words became at least seven steps. An expert skips steps because they seem obvious; a manual must not.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Accidents come from hurry and carelessness</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Experts make “sometimes grievous mistakes, irreversible mistakes”. Novices make more mistakes, but theirs can usually be put right. A famous saying: “accidents happen when you are either in a hurry or careless”. The lecture links the Titanic, Chernobyl and Bhopal to “somebody not paying adequate attention to his part of the … process and the language related to it”. Exactness means “only one nut will fit there. We must know which nut and where and when.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-manual-making-tea\">The lecture’s manual: making tea</h3>\n<p>The slides <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=952s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:52</a>. (The second slide starts its letters again from <em>e</em>; here the steps are numbered straight through.)</p>\n<p><strong>Ingredients:</strong> a cup of water; half a tea-spoonful of tea leaves.</p>\n<p><strong>Steps:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Take a pan or kettle or percolator to boil water.</li>\n<li>Pour a cup of water into it.</li>\n<li>Keep the kettle/pan on stove.</li>\n<li>Light the stove.</li>\n<li>Let the pan … be there until water in it boils.</li>\n<li>Meanwhile warm the tea-pot, keeping it near the stove.</li>\n<li>Put the tea leaves in the pot.</li>\n<li>After bubble settles down in the boiled water, pour this hot water gently upon the pot, such that the falling water does not break the leaves.</li>\n<li>Let the leaves soak water and sink at the bottom of the pot.</li>\n<li>After the leaves sink, pour tea through a strainer into your cup.</li>\n<li>Tea may look yellow or red. And will be ready for drinking.</li>\n<li>If you like your tea with sugar and milk, or honey and lime, then you can add them to your tea according to your taste at this stage.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A <strong>percolator</strong> is a pot for boiling water (and making coffee); a <strong>strainer</strong> is a small sieve that holds back the leaves. The lecturer’s remarks: unbroken leaves (not dust or broken leaves) give the best tea; many people add spices, honey, lemon or ginger, but quite a few like it plain, “tea for the sake of tea”; if you are allergic to tea, ask a doctor first. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a></p>\n<p>The words the lecturer points to, by kind <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Words in the tea manual</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Things</td>\n<td>pan, kettle, percolator, stove, tea-pot, strainer, cup; water, tea leaves, sugar, milk, honey, lime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Actions</td>\n<td>take, pour, keep, light, boil, warm, put (or <em>add</em>; with a tea bag, <em>dip</em>), soak, sink</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Place</td>\n<td>into it, on the stove, near the stove, at the bottom of the pot, through a strainer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sequence and duration</td>\n<td>until water in it boils, meanwhile, after bubble settles down, after the leaves sink, at this stage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Changes</td>\n<td>the bubbles settle; the leaves soak, grow heavy and sink; the water turns yellow or red</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“There are words that indicate action, there are words that indicate sequence, and there are words that indicate duration. A lab manual must take care of all of these things in a sequence.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Wait for the sign</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Let the leaves soak” is a duration in disguise. The leaves do not sink the moment the water falls on them: they first float, soak up water, become heavy and then settle. The manual tells you to wait for this sign. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-model-preparing-coffee\">The lecture’s model: preparing coffee</h3>\n<p>The activity <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=1253s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:53</a>: “In a similar manner, write a manual on how to prepare coffee”: basic black coffee. Then check your manual for “processes, combination of processes, duration of processes and sequence of processes”. The model answer <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=1284s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:24</a>:</p>\n<p><strong>Ingredients:</strong> one and a half cups of water; three tablespoons of pure coffee powder or coffee-chicory blend.</p>\n<p><strong>Steps:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Take a pan or kettle to boil water.</li>\n<li>Pour one and a half cups of water into it.</li>\n<li>Place the pan/kettle on a stove.</li>\n<li>Light the stove.</li>\n<li>Keep the pan/kettle on the stove until the water in it boils.</li>\n<li>Meanwhile, attach the top and bottom portion of a coffee filter.</li>\n<li>Add three tablespoons of coffee powder/blend to the top portion.</li>\n<li>Press the powder down and form a firm bed of coffee granules.</li>\n<li>Once bubbles settle down in the boiled water, gently pour some of the hot water into the top portion of the filter over the bed of coffee granules.</li>\n<li>Keep pouring hot water till it reaches a little below the rim of the filter.</li>\n<li>Immediately close the filter with its lid and secure it.</li>\n<li>Coffee decoction will collect in the bottom part of the filter.</li>\n<li>Pour the remaining hot water in a coffee mug and set it aside.</li>\n<li>After 5 minutes, carefully detach the bottom part of the coffee filter and pour the coffee decoction into the coffee mug with hot water that was set aside.</li>\n<li>Black coffee will be ready for consumption.</li>\n<li>Milk, jaggery, sugar and/or cinnamon can be added to this based on preference.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(A <strong>decoction</strong> is the strong coffee liquid that drips through the powder.)</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Checking the coffee manual against the lecture’s list</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Things:</strong> pan or kettle, stove, coffee filter (top portion, bottom portion, lid), coffee mug; water, coffee powder or blend.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> take, pour, place, light, keep, attach, add, press, close, secure, detach.</li>\n<li><strong>Sequence:</strong> <em>meanwhile</em> (step 6), <em>once</em> (step 9), <em>immediately</em> (step 11), <em>after</em> (step 14).</li>\n<li><strong>Duration:</strong> <em>until the water in it boils</em> (step 5), <em>till it reaches a little below the rim</em> (step 10), <em>after 5 minutes</em> (step 14).</li>\n<li><strong>Changes:</strong> bubbles settle down (step 9); the decoction collects in the bottom part (step 12).</li>\n<li><strong>Quantities:</strong> one and a half cups of water, three tablespoons of powder.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> every kind of word the lecture asks for is there, so a beginner can follow it without guessing.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"writing-to-influence\">Writing to influence</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“What is influencing others? When everyone believes that you are right, when everyone does what you do, when everyone wants to do what you do even when they cannot.” The slide shows a flock of white birds flying in formation behind one black bird, under the words “The power of influence”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"is-it-right-to-influence\">Is it right to influence?</h3>\n<p>“Is it ethically correct to influence others?” The lecturer calls it “a very highly delicate ethical question”. Many of us believe in what we write and want others to believe it too, and “in that sense, it is not unethical”. So the lecture’s question is not <em>whether</em> we should influence, but <strong>what language</strong> people use when they do. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Writing to influence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Writing that tries to make readers think, decide and believe as the writer does. It is the daily work of advertising agencies and of “propaganda, rhetoric, politics”: of “those who are in the business of influencing mass opinion”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Propaganda</strong> is writing or speech that pushes one view on a mass of people, often by choosing or bending the facts.</li>\n<li><strong>Rhetoric</strong> is the art of persuading through language.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"words-that-hide-quantity\">Words that hide quantity</h3>\n<p>The slide’s list <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=214s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:34</a>: <em>finally, how many, recent weeks, numerous, several, a strong preference, continuing to rise</em>. These are “words indicating and questioning quantity” that never give it. The lecturer’s questions:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Words</th>\n<th>The question to ask</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>recent weeks</em></td>\n<td>How recent? Yesterday? Last week?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>numerous, several</em></td>\n<td>How many?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>a strong preference</em></td>\n<td>“Can we quantify strong?”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>continuing to rise</em></td>\n<td>“Where was it and how much it rose to?”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Without these “specific particulars”, such words fill documents that seek to influence you. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"percentages-without-people-the-surveys\">Percentages without people: the surveys</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=284s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:44</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In an Auto survey last week, 78.1% of the total 350 respondents showed preference towards personal car or two-wheeler. Cabs were preferred by 8.7% while only 7.2% voted for buses or metro. The remaining voted for autos.</p>\n<p>Another survey included over 400 middle and high-income groups in Delhi and surrounding NCR cities. The analysis showed that preference for car ownership and usage will increase in the short run.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A <strong>respondent</strong> is a person who answers a survey; the <strong>sample</strong> is the group of people asked. What the lecturer points out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>number</strong> of people, 350, appears once; after that come only <strong>percentages</strong>, because “78 percent otherwise sounds great”. Yet a single big city such as Hyderabad has lakhs of car owners. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></li>\n<li>“<strong>The remaining</strong> voted for autos” hides how small that group is. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></li>\n<li>The second survey gives no percentages; instead it describes its <strong>sample</strong>: middle- and high-income groups in Delhi and the National Capital Region (Ghaziabad, Faridabad, Noida, Gurgaon and others). “You soon forget that only 400 people were interviewed … what you remember is so many cities.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a></li>\n<li>Its conclusion is full of <strong>tentative</strong> (careful, not certain) words: <em>preference</em> (they would like a car; nobody says they bought one), <em>ownership and usage</em> (owning a car is not the same as using it), <em>in the short run</em> (“How long, three months, two months, ten weeks, twelve weeks?”). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a></li>\n<li>The chart of the first survey shows only percentages; the 350 appears only in small print at the foot (“Sample size: 350 people”). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=730s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:10</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">How many people is “78.1%”? (the lecture’s chart at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=730s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:10</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The chart: private vehicles 78.1%, taxis 8.7%, autos 6%, bus/metro 7.2%; sample size 350 people.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Add the three percentages the text gives: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">78.1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8.7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7.2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">94.0</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li>“The remaining” voted for autos: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">94.0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6.0</span></span></span></span>,</span> that is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6%</span></span></span></span>,</span> as the chart shows.</li>\n<li>Private vehicles: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">78.1%</span></span></span></span></span> of <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span></span></span></span></span> is <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.781</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">273.35</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> about 273 people.</li>\n<li>Taxis: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.087</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30.45</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> about 30 people.</li>\n<li>Bus/metro: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.072</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25.2</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> about 25 people.</li>\n<li>Autos: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.06</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">21</span></span></span></span></span> people.</li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">273.35</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30.45</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25.2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">21</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the impressive “78.1%” is about 273 people, and each other choice is between 21 and 30 people. (The lecturer estimates these aloud and rounds loosely; the exact values are above.) A percentage means little until you know <strong>of how many</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The next slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=775s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:55</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Another survey stated that 62% respondents had said they would avoid cab-hailing firms. This survey had a sample size of 3,000 and covered categories including shopping habits, purchase habits, travel, social activities, investments, social responsibilities during this pandemic.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecturer: “62 percent of how many? Should you not first say” how many people were asked? This survey gives both the number and the percentages, and asks about many parts of life, so it looks “more objective” than the others. But “look at the reasons behind people stating it”: it too is “seeking to influence the reader”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></p>\n<p>The slide after that <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=894s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:54</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>several factors,</li>\n<li>Preference</li>\n<li>may fall</li>\n<li>36% respondents who do not own any vehicle, around 28% said they may want to buy one near future. Yet, 73% respondents have preferred….if it meets high quality standards.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecturer: “30 percent of how many? If it is 30 percent of the global population, then you have great business, go ahead. But if you have 30 percent of the population of Maldives, then please work on the image of your brand.” And: “what do you mean by high quality standards?” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">“28%” of whom?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Ask what each percentage is a percentage <strong>of</strong>. “Around 28% said they may want to buy one”: 28% of all the respondents, or 28% of the 36% who own no vehicle? If it is the second, it is only <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.28</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">36%</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10%</span></span></span></span> of all the respondents. (This example is mine; the lecture’s question is the same: “of how many?”)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">How to spot writing that seeks to influence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Using vague words, giving impression of presenting big things when you are actually presenting small things or giving the impression that you are presenting small things, when you are presenting big things”: these mark “a propaganda piece of writing, seeking to influence”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Keep the antenna up</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“If you keep that antenna, that filter in your mind”, you will see the propaganda part of a text “in no time”. Ask: <strong>Of how many? How recent? How long? What standards? Who was asked?</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Even “we are the best” influences</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Critical use of vague words as keywords also influences our opinion.” And: “every time we say we are the best, we are seeking to influence others rather than letting them reach their own conclusions.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-practice-report-and-essay\">The practice report and essay</h3>\n<p>The activity <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=970s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:10</a>: first, analyse a report and list the words and expressions that seek to influence the reader’s opinion; second, write about 200 words on “Should there be a censor certification for films? There are opinions both for and against. What are these opinions? What do you prefer? Why?”</p>\n<p>The report, shown briefly at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=1049s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:29</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>There is despair and anger on the roads of large metros in India. Even though the strike by hundreds of thousands of drivers of app-based cab service providers Ola and Uber was called off by February end, the frustration and sense of betrayal persists. This is likely to continue as these drivers are faced with political and economic forces much beyond their imagination. They are owners of capital (their vehicles). And yet, the embrace of global capital, which they hoped would help them leapfrog over class inequalities, has landed them in a trap.</p>\n<p>App-based taxi services are a clever use of the modern smartphone coupled with blazing fast mobile internet access. Their success, world over, is generally attributed to providing a better service in comparison to old-style taxi services. This perception is true, but also leaves out the crucial role played by the neoliberal political economy. The defining markers of the latter are first, an utter reluctance by the state to regulate the sphere of emerging social relations, and second, the push towards a debt-based economy to keep the game of finance capital rolling.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Words that seek to influence in the report (my analysis; the lecture shows no answer slide)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Feelings stated as facts:</strong> <em>despair and anger</em>, <em>the frustration and sense of betrayal persists</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Big numbers without a count:</strong> <em>hundreds of thousands of drivers</em>, <em>large metros</em>, <em>world over</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Tentative words:</strong> <em>likely to continue</em>, <em>generally attributed</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exaggeration:</strong> <em>much beyond their imagination</em>, <em>blazing fast</em>, <em>utter reluctance</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pictures (metaphors) that judge:</strong> <em>leapfrog over class inequalities</em>, <em>landed them in a trap</em>, <em>keep the game of finance capital rolling</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Steering words:</strong> <em>And yet</em>; <em>This perception is true, but also leaves out the crucial role…</em> The writer grants a point, then turns the reader to their own view.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> almost every sentence carries a word chosen to make the reader feel for the drivers and distrust “global capital”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The model answer for the essay <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=1052s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:32</a> has four paragraphs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Situation:</strong> films “both draw from reality and seek to deviate from it”; Indian films “have always undergone a process of censor certification and there are many arguments both for and against this practice”.</li>\n<li><em><strong>On the one hand</strong></em> (against censorship): artists everywhere claim the right to show reality through exaggeration, and “numerous directors” have refused to cut scenes. “<em><strong>Moreover</strong></em>, censorship and democracy do not go hand-in-hand. <em><strong>If</strong></em> people have full and unquestioned freedom to cast their vote for the party of their choice, … <em><strong>then</strong></em> the idea that the content they consume must be mediated by the state is jarring.”</li>\n<li><em><strong>On the other hand</strong></em>: the certification board demands the removal of scenes “considered objectionable”, weighs the public’s reaction and the tensions in civil society, and “in some cases” follows “an out-dated and hypocritical notion of morality”.</li>\n<li><strong>Conclusion</strong> (<em><strong>While</strong></em> …): censorship “has little place in a democracy”, but <strong>certification ratings</strong> are needed for “age-appropriate content”, so that parents, guardians and caregivers can decide what their children watch.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two things to notice in this model answer (my notes)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>It uses the structure and the argument words of Lectures 4.1–4.2: <em>On the one hand, On the other hand, Moreover, If … then, While</em>.</li>\n<li>It also uses influencing words of the kind Lecture 4.4 warns about: <em>numerous</em>, <em>jarring</em>, <em>vocally challenged</em>, <em>overwhelming reaction</em>, <em>out-dated and hypocritical</em>, “an idea that requires a speedy death”. And it runs to about 344 words for a 200-word task; Lecture 4.6 shows how to cut.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture ends <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>: “language is at the heart of all enterprise that mankind has done”. Know <strong>why</strong> you are using language (what you want to achieve) and <strong>how</strong> to use it. “If you do so deliberately, no problem, but if you do so involuntarily, without realizing” that you are influencing others “rather than present all facts before them and let them think themselves, you have been guilty of that.” The lesson is meant to help you overcome such biases.</p>\n<h2 id=\"essays-on-reflection-planning-with-a-synopsis\">Essays on reflection: planning with a synopsis</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>We write for many purposes: to tell stories, to seek and give information, to share feelings and ideas, to describe, to discuss options, and to <strong>reflect</strong> on “philosophical subjects” that “concern us all across time” and have “no clear black or white answers”. We reflect on them, find our own answers “and we live accordingly”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reflective essay</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An essay on a topic that has <strong>no single right answer</strong>. You think the topic through, look at its different sides, support your thinking with ideas, examples and evidence, and present your own view. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s topic <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=118s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:58</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A lot of people say all human beings are alike. They can, therefore, live and work alike. We need not worry about local cultures, local languages. One global culture will create global happiness. Do you agree? Write an essay in about 200 words presenting your views on the subject. You can use any ideas, examples and evidence in support.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Topics in exams</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In many South Asian school exams the topic is a word or two: “write an essay in about 200 words on happiness”. Standard language tests worldwide state the topic clearly, with its <strong>dimensions</strong> (the factors you should take into account), as above. If you get a bare topic, the principles are the same. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A good essay needs more than knowledge of the world and of the language: it depends on <strong>how</strong> you present what you know. An experienced chef was asked how his cooking differs from a roadside joint’s. “Not very much,” he said: the difference is that a big restaurant cleans its tools and utensils every day. “We do it with perfection, we do it in a system.” An essay needs the same system. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"do-not-start-writing-at-once\">Do not start writing at once</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:10</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Do not start writing at once.</li>\n<li>Create a synopsis of relevant points.</li>\n<li>You can also think of examples, illustrations, different kinds of evidence and ideas to further clarify the subject, as given below.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>“As soon as you get the topic, no matter what you know already … sit down, hold yourself and first create a synopsis of relevant points.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a></li>\n<li>Keep it to about a paragraph, as <strong>hints</strong>, not in full sentences. It is your private paper and will not be marked, so spelling, punctuation and grammar do not matter there, as long as you understand your own points. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a></li>\n<li>Only <strong>relevant</strong> points: an essay on happiness is not the place for cows, dogs or pets. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></li>\n<li>Professional essayists (the lecture names Aldous Huxley among others) “think before they write”, and organise their facts so that the plan stays with them while they write. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-a-synopsis-is\">What a synopsis is</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Synopsis</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=768s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:48</a>: “A synopsis is an <strong>overview</strong>, like you stand on the rooftop and look at the entire city or village.” From the roof you see the whole city, but not its details. A synopsis is an overview of what you are going to say, “not necessarily in the same order”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer’s joke <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>: a tourist wanted to see all of New York for 17 dollars. A taxi driver offered to show him all of it for 5. He took the 5 dollars and said: go up to the top of the 104-floor tower (the World Trade Center), and you will see all of New York. Height gives you the whole view: that is what a synopsis does.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Half the job</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Once we make a synopsis, half the job is done.” “For a good essay, good synopsis is important.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-synopsis-on-happiness\">The lecture’s synopsis on happiness</h3>\n<p>For the “one global culture will create global happiness” topic, the lecture’s synopsis <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=526s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:46</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Definition of happiness – instances of happiness – empathy – cut-throat competition – work life – statistical evidence – working hours – English language for job application – number of suicides – mental and physical health etc.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>New words: <strong>empathy</strong> is understanding and sharing another person’s feelings (the lecture sets it against <strong>antipathy</strong>, a strong dislike); <strong>cut-throat competition</strong> is very fierce competition. How the lecturer opens up the points <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Define</strong> the subject: “What do you mean by happiness?” You cannot hand a friend “3 kilograms of happiness”; happiness differs from person to person. Even “eating fish” raises questions: fish from where, cooked by whom, served by whom? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>Instances</strong>: moments when we were happy or unhappy; “maybe every birth made us happier, and every death made us unhappier”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Cut-throat competition</strong>, and how life and work fit together: does competition mean that only the very rich or very successful can compete? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Statistical evidence</strong>, perhaps at the national level, and examples from beyond your own place. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"why-the-five-minutes-are-worth-it\">Why the five minutes are worth it</h3>\n<p>The slides <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=961s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:01</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1037s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:17</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>A good essay for reflection cannot include everything, but it must dwell upon some important things.</li>\n<li>Designing a good synopsis can help you write the best essay you are capable of writing.</li>\n<li>If you have only 30 minutes to write an essay, as in many national and international tests, spend at least five minutes writing a synopsis.</li>\n<li>This will save your time, it will help you produce as good an essay as you are really capable of.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>What you want to say “is already there in your head” (the lecturer recalls Vivekananda: our abilities are hidden within us). The synopsis brings it out at the right moment, so you write with <strong>felicity</strong> (ease) and in fewer words, instead of wandering “in a dark room, not knowing which way to go”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Synopsis on top, one paragraph per point</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many school systems let you write the synopsis at the top of the page. Then develop <strong>one paragraph for each point</strong> of it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-partisan-synopsis-and-a-better-one\">A partisan synopsis, and a better one</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s Activity 1 <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1135s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:55</a>: make a synopsis for “Should one have unlimited freedom? Should there be no censorship for mass media. Write an essay in about 200 words presenting your views on the subject.”</p>\n<p>Synopsis-1 <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1195s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:55</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Print media – electronic media – new media – movies – creative freedom – freedom of press – surveillance – certification – emergence of online streaming sites post Covid</p>\n</blockquote>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Partisan</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Partisan</strong> means taking one side only. A <strong>partisan essay</strong> argues for one side, or covers only one part of the topic, and leaves out the rest, by accident or on purpose. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s comments <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1281s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:21</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Many of us do not look at all possible aspects of a topic that makes only a partisan essay.</li>\n<li>A good synopsis should reflect such a comprehensive thought.</li>\n<li>In the synopsis given above, the writer intends to say nearly all about censorship in public life; but the writer says nothing about individual – should one have unlimited freedom.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The better synopsis <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1515s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:15</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Concept of absolute freedom and relative freedom — meaning of freedom for an individual in society with regards to education, religion, language, movement, job, partner — difficulty of absolute freedom in certain areas such as speech — highlight the necessity of relative freedom here in order for peaceful co-existence</p>\n<p>Different sub-categories of mass media — their functions — print media, social media, radio, television, other electronic media — creative freedom — democracy — certification process</p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part of the topic</th>\n<th>Synopsis-1</th>\n<th>The better synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>The individual’s freedom</td>\n<td>missing</td>\n<td>absolute and relative freedom; education, religion, language, movement, job, partner; limits on speech</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mass media</td>\n<td>print, electronic, new media, movies, streaming</td>\n<td>the sub-categories and their functions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Censorship</td>\n<td>freedom of press, surveillance, certification</td>\n<td>creative freedom, democracy, the certification process</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The model answer <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>, about 245 words, follows the better synopsis point by point:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Freedom was long understood as <strong>absolute</strong>, “with no limitations whatsoever”. “<em><strong>However</strong></em>, in any civil society, <strong>relative</strong> freedom is necessary for peaceful co-existence.”</li>\n<li>For the individual, freedom means choices: “education, religion, language, movement, job, and partner”; in these matters, “unlimited freedom is a right”.</li>\n<li>In <strong>speech</strong>, absolute freedom is “curtailed” (cut down) to relative freedom, because absolute freedom “would entail hate speech, threats, and verbal abuse”.</li>\n<li>For the <strong>mass media</strong>, censoring is “an undemocratic principle”. “<em><strong>Rather</strong></em>, a certification process to determine kinds of content for categorisation purposes is needed.”</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">One-sided by accident</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Even without meaning to, we can end up with a partisan essay by missing part of the topic; the lecturer recalls the same danger in précis writing. After making the synopsis, “go back to the topic” and check that every part of it has a point. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Some people collect points “alphabetically” (an idea for A, then B, then C); others go straight to examples. “Without examples, your synopsis may not have the same strength.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a> The lecture’s last word: “a good essay requires good preparation”. Before you write, jog your memory and ask: how much do I know on this subject, and how much of it is relevant? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"essays-on-reflection-time-stories-cohesion-and-drafts\">Essays on reflection: time, stories, cohesion and drafts</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 4.6 recalls three kinds of essay: essays that <strong>describe</strong> things, essays that <strong>discuss two sides</strong> of a controversial issue, and essays that <strong>reflect</strong> on issues with no one definite answer, like choosing between the road that branches left and the road that branches right. Higher-level tests set the third kind; for example, Task 2 of the writing module of <strong>IELTS</strong> (the International English Language Testing System). None of us is good at everything, so we follow <strong>steps</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"plan-your-30-minutes\">Plan your 30 minutes</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A 200-word essay in 30 minutes (the lecture’s plan at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> 5 minutes for the synopsis: points connected with little hyphens (instances, evidence, ideas, statistics, the definition and the dimensions of the topic).</li>\n<li><strong>Write:</strong> 20 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Revise:</strong> 5 minutes.</li>\n<li>Total: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30</span></span></span></span></span> minutes.</li>\n<li>Planning and revising take <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span></span></span></span></span> minutes, which is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">30</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">10</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span> of the time; writing takes <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">30</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">20</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li>Speed needed: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span></span></span></span></span> words a minute, “which is a lot of good time”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 5 + 20 + 5 minutes. In the lecture’s summary: “spend one-third of the time planning the essay and revising the essay and in two thirds writing the essay” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Revision</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=191s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:11</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A good essay is not possible without revision.</li>\n<li>Revision will help you get rid of those little mistakes that happened because of oversight, because of haste and because of slip of hand, etc.</li>\n<li>On the whole, if you have 30 minutes for an essay, you should keep at least five minutes for revising it.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>These slips happen not because we do not know the subject or the language, but because we are busy with the content: “You may think you have written E, but it could become I … T, but it could become I or L … a V, as in victory, but it may become U as an umbrella.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"stories-make-your-essay-different\">Stories make your essay different</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=372s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:12</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>A good essay must also have some stories, anecdotes, etc.</li>\n<li>This story can be included in synopsis in the form of instances and illustrations. While writing at least about 20-25 percent of time and words may be given to illustrations, instances from your experience.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For a 200-word essay written in 20 minutes, that is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">40</span></span></span></span></span> to <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.25</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50</span></span></span></span></span> words, and about 4 to 5 minutes (my arithmetic on the slide’s figure).</p>\n<p>Why stories? Everyone makes a synopsis, everyone revises, everyone uses statistics, and everyone quotes the same lines: Aristotle’s “man is a social animal”, Rousseau on freedom as a basic right. Your own story is the one thing others do not have. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A story that makes an essay (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A young man’s statement of purpose described the most challenging moment of his life:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>He worked in a mining company in Australia.</li>\n<li>He noticed that his boss, the store manager, deliberately bought things at a higher price from one supplier and kept the extra money.</li>\n<li>Nobody had asked him to keep watch. Should he tell senior management, or keep quiet?</li>\n<li>He gathered courage and told them. An inquiry found the charge true.</li>\n<li>He was promoted and given protection; the manager was fired.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> in an essay on honesty, this one story makes the essay “richer, better, more meaningful” than many others, because “not all of us have the same stories”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Synopsis as a budget</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A synopsis is your plan for how to write an essay, just as you make a budget.” A student on a small allowance plans so much for rent, so much for travel, so much for the phone, and manages meals on what is left: say 40 rupees out of 100. In the same way, a synopsis shares out your words: some points get two paragraphs, some one, some half a paragraph. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Personal anecdotes are welcome, but only “such personal anecdotes that we can share in public”: that decision is yours. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">These are not rules</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Nobody can say you must do these things … The word here is you can.” They work in combination: grammatically correct language and appropriate words (grammar, vocabulary, punctuation and spelling), a good synopsis, good anecdotes or stories, and evidence both personal and statistical “will give you a great essay”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"cohesion-the-glue-of-an-essay\">Cohesion: the glue of an essay</h3>\n<p>Writing from a synopsis has one danger: sentences that sit next to the bullet points but do not connect. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Cohesion</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=796s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:16</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The final draft must be easy and interesting to read.</li>\n<li>Sentences and paragraphs there must not remain unconnected.</li>\n<li>One must lead to the other, the other must follow from the preceding, which means you should use lots of cohesive devices connecting sentences and paragraphs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cohesion</strong> is this connectedness. A <strong>cohesive device</strong> is a word that links a sentence or paragraph to another: a connector (discourse marker), a pronoun or a preposition.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The devices on the slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=878s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:38</a>: <em>as, example, for example, here, henceforth, hence, illustrating, nevertheless, so, however, howsoever, thus, therefore, that is why</em>, etc. (the lecturer adds <em>regardless</em>). “Using these markers, using prepositions properly, using pronouns only after nouns, etc. strings the sentences into meaningful paragraph.” Discourse markers were introduced in <a href=\"#/english2/w01/discourse-markers\">Week 1</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Device</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n<th>Without it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Preposition</td>\n<td>Freedom is the basic need <strong>of</strong> every human being.</td>\n<td>“the basic need <strong>on</strong> every human being” confuses; only <em>of</em> or <em>for</em> fits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pronoun after its noun</td>\n<td>Saints have said that sacrificing, giving away, also brings happiness. <strong>They</strong> have also said that ultimate happiness lies in disowning things rather than owning them.</td>\n<td>repeating “saints have said that …” in every sentence is “monotonous … boring”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connector</td>\n<td><em>thus, therefore, however, that is why</em></td>\n<td>sentences stay isolated, like notes next to bullet points</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Bricks and mortar</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A building is not bricks kept one upon another: they are joined with mortar and iron bars, and columns and beams hold each other up. “Similarly, here in an essay, these discourse markers keep sentences together, and it is the sentences that keep discourse markers together.” Without them, an essay is “a loose body of isolated sentences”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"drafts-the-happiness-essay\">Drafts: the happiness essay</h3>\n<p>“No matter how clever one is, it is seldom that the first draft itself is the best draft.” The second is better than the first, the third better still, and by the fifth or sixth “we know that this is the best I could have done”. After a few months, or a semester or two, it becomes a habit, and then even one or two drafts are good. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a></p>\n<p>The activity repeats the topic of Lecture 4.5 (“all human beings are alike … one global culture will create global happiness. Do you agree?”) with the same happiness synopsis <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=1218s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:18</a>. Draft 1 <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=1225s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:25</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The definition of happiness is different for every individual. While some may consider being close to nature gives them happiness. Others may think that splurging on shopping brings happiness to them. The United Nations presents a World Happiness report through a survey. The survey includes various parameters such as mental illness, diversity, communication, work, food and shelter, etc. According to the report, Finland continues to be the happiest country for the third consecutive time.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The next slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=1245s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:45</a>: “We seldom get perfect work in the first draft itself.” There are exceptions (“In India we say Brihaspati was born reciting Vedas”; “many people say Shakespeare did not revise anything he wrote”), but most people learn by trial and error.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">One slip to fix in Draft 1 (my note)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“While some may consider being close to nature gives them happiness.” is not a full sentence: a <em>while</em>-clause is a subordinate clause and cannot stand alone (<a href=\"#/english2/w03/complex-sentences-and-the-kinds-of-clause\">Week 3</a>). Join it to the next sentence: <em>While some may consider that being close to nature gives them happiness, others may think that splurging on shopping brings happiness to them.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Draft 2 <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=1284s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:24</a> is a new and much fuller essay in four paragraphs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Cultures.</strong> It opens with a saying: “God made country, man made towns, goes a saying in English.” One world was divided into many cultures “because of our convenience or because of our fear or pride”; and “because all human beings are differently inclined, different cultures have emerged”.</li>\n<li><strong>Local culture, and the definition.</strong> Cultures took “hundreds of thousands of years” to evolve, “but they can be destroyed in no time”. A personal story: “The temple at my village is just a lump of clay under a tree but that lump of clay has protected the entire village for centuries, as the villagers believe.” Then the synopsis’s <strong>definition</strong>: happiness is “a state of mind free from pain and anxieties”, or, positively, “getting a desired thing or experience”.</li>\n<li><strong>Instances.</strong> A child is happy with his mother, young people with their friends, old people with their children or grandchildren. A personal anecdote: “I eat 21 meals a week but I have a friend who eats only four meals a week and I think he is happier than I am.” So “happiness cannot have one definition”.</li>\n<li><strong>Competition and health.</strong> Everyone wants “the best of everything without waiting for long”. “<em><strong>As a result</strong></em>”, acquiring more and more “is detrimental to happiness”; “<em><strong>Consequently</strong></em>”, health problems caused by stress have risen, and suicides occur among people “plain bored with what they have”. It ends: “Happiness as sages have said <em><strong>finally</strong></em> lies in sharing both our problems and privileges.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p>“Compare the two”: you will see which words have gone and which sentences have changed <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>. Draft 2 draws on the synopsis (definition, instances, cut-throat competition, health, suicides), adds the stories that this lecture asks for, and links its sentences with cohesive devices (<em>But, Because, As a result, Consequently, that is why, finally</em>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Fix these while revising Draft 2 (my notes)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>“Its one world” → “<strong>It’s</strong> one world” (<em>it’s</em> = <em>it is</em>); “Today’s world everyone wants” → “<strong>In</strong> today’s world, everyone wants”.</li>\n<li>“the American constitution said that each person has the right to pursue their happiness”: the phrase “the pursuit of happiness” comes from the American <strong>Declaration of Independence</strong> (1776), not the Constitution. Check facts and quotations before an essay leans on them.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"cutting-to-the-word-limit\">Cutting to the word limit</h3>\n<p>“You might count, and you might see that the draft … has more than 200 words.” Draft 2 has 444 words, so <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">444</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">244</span></span></span></span></span> words must go. The lecture’s advice <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the first draft, do not worry much about length. That does not mean writing 2,000 words where 200 are asked, but 300 is no problem: you are coming back anyway.</li>\n<li>When revising, remove words that are <strong>redundant</strong> (not needed), replace a group of words with <strong>one word</strong>, and <strong>rewrite</strong> sentences shorter: “these 3 or 4 standard things, as editors do”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Three cuts on Draft 2 (my examples, using the lecture’s methods)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Remove redundant words.</strong> “But because of our convenience or because of our fear or pride, we divided this world into many cultures and countries.” (21 words) → “But because of our convenience, fear or pride, we divided this world into many cultures and countries.” (17 words). Saved: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">21</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">17</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li><strong>One word for many.</strong> “But they can be destroyed in no time.” (8 words) → “But they can be destroyed instantly.” (6 words). Saved: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li><strong>Rewrite the sentence.</strong> “Somebody is happy working all the time living in great comfort, enjoying great facilities. Some others are happy doing nothing or away from all facilities and living on the minimum.” (30 words) → “Some are happy working all the time in great comfort; others, doing nothing and living on the minimum.” (18 words). Saved: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">18</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">12</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> three small edits save <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">18</span></span></span></span></span> words and keep the meaning. Draft 2 is 244 words over, so the same work must go through every sentence, and some examples may have to go.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s closing summary <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>: follow the steps. The synopsis should include “a definition, instances, illustration, a statistical evidence, … the different sides of the story”. Then write, then revise, giving one-third of the time to planning and revising and two-thirds to writing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Argue (4.1):</strong> present two sides or more; policies are rarely undisputed. Build content from what you know, local examples, statistics and anecdotes, and related cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Language of argument (4.1):</strong> <em>on one hand … the other, as far as, as well as, point of view, in my view, in view of, on considering</em>; comparison (<em>least, most, more, less</em>); adverbs (<em>actually</em>); nouns for groups (<em>majority, half the population</em>); expressions demanding consideration (<em>before you conclude, while we are at it, one can as well see, in totality</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Structure (4.2):</strong> situation → one side → the other side (third or fourth paragraph) → overview and an acceptable conclusion. Statistics for policy, anecdotes among friends and family, mixed “like salt in sambar”.</li>\n<li><strong>Sentences (4.2):</strong> complex sentences with <em>If … then, While, Whereas, As … so</em>; simple sentences in contrast (<em>Stars twinkle, planets do not</em>). The story test: argument words would not be in a story.</li>\n<li><strong>Lab manual (4.3):</strong> a recipe; exact words for things and actions (nouns, verbs), sequence, duration and changes. Never skip “obvious” steps; accidents come from hurry and carelessness.</li>\n<li><strong>Influence (4.4):</strong> vague quantity words (<em>numerous, several, recent weeks, a strong preference, continuing to rise</em>), percentages without their base, impressive descriptions of small samples, tentative words (<em>preference, in the short run</em>). Ask: of how many? how recent? how long? what standards?</li>\n<li><strong>Reflective essay (4.5):</strong> no single right answer. Do not start at once; make a synopsis, an overview from the rooftop, of relevant points: definition, instances, evidence, stories and <strong>all</strong> parts of the topic (not partisan).</li>\n<li><strong>Thirty minutes (4.6):</strong> 5 synopsis + 20 writing + 5 revising (one-third planning and revising, two-thirds writing); 10 words a minute; 20–25% of the words for stories and instances.</li>\n<li><strong>Cohesion and drafts (4.6):</strong> connectors (<em>however, therefore, thus, hence, nevertheless, that is why</em>), correct prepositions, pronouns after their nouns. Write several drafts; cut redundant words, use one word for many, rewrite long sentences.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A report says: “Half the population in metro cities never works.” Which kind of argument word is “half the population”, and what should a careful reader ask?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>It is a <strong>noun for a group</strong>, one of the five kinds of argument words in Lecture 4.1: it shows the size of a section of opinion but gives no count. Ask: <strong>half of whom, and how many people were actually counted?</strong> In the lecturer’s soap example, “half the people we surveyed” might be 250 out of 500 people asked, in a country of 1,000 million.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Put these parts of an argument essay in the usual order. P: arguments for the other side. Q: an overview and an acceptable conclusion. R: a statement of the situation. S: arguments for one side. Then: in the model essay on big chain stores, which part begins “While everyone that is part of the global market may benefit…”?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Order: R, S, P, Q.</strong> This is the slide’s structure: the situation first, then one side, then (in the third or fourth paragraph) the other side, and finally an overview with a conclusion.</p>\n<p>The paragraph beginning “While everyone … may benefit” is <strong>P, the other side</strong>: it is the third paragraph, and <em>While</em> turns the argument against the benefits listed in paragraph 2.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">An expert’s manual says: “Boil the water and make the tea.” Using the lecture’s four kinds of words, what is missing?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Compare it with the lecture’s tea manual:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Things:</strong> which vessel (pan, kettle or percolator), how much water (a cup), how much tea (half a tea-spoonful of leaves), a tea-pot, a strainer, a cup.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> pour, keep on the stove, light, warm the tea-pot, put the leaves in the pot, pour gently, pour through a strainer.</li>\n<li><strong>Sequence and duration:</strong> <em>until</em> the water boils; <em>meanwhile</em> warm the pot; <em>after</em> the bubbles settle; let the leaves soak <em>until</em> they sink.</li>\n<li><strong>Changes:</strong> the bubbles settle, the leaves sink, the tea looks yellow or red.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The expert has skipped everything that seemed obvious: the mistake the lecture warns about (“cut it, fry it”).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A report says “62% of respondents would avoid cab-hailing firms”, and only later that the sample was 3,000 people. How many people is that, and what does putting the percentage first do?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">62%</span></span></span></span></span> of <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span></span></span></span></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.62</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">860</span></span></span></span> people.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Giving the percentage first lets the big-sounding “62%” make its impression before the reader can ask “<strong>of how many?</strong>”. The lecturer’s comment: “Should you not first say” how many respondents there were?</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">You have 30 minutes for a 200-word reflective essay. Plan your time, your writing speed and the share for stories.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Synopsis 5 minutes, writing 20 minutes, revision 5 minutes: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Writing speed: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span></span></span></span></span> words a minute.</li>\n<li>Stories and instances, 20–25% of the words: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">40</span></span></span></span></span> to <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.25</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50</span></span></span></span></span> words, about 4 to 5 of the 20 writing minutes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 5 + 20 + 5 minutes, 10 words a minute, and about 40–50 words of stories, as Lecture 4.6 advises.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Fill each gap with a cohesive device, and name its kind. (i) “Freedom is the basic need ___ every human being.” (ii) “Saints have said that sharing brings happiness. ___ have also said that ultimate happiness lies in disowning things.” (iii) “Everyone wants the best of everything without waiting for long. ___, there is a never ending emphasis upon acquiring more and more.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(i) <strong>of</strong> (or <em>for</em>), a <strong>preposition</strong>. “The basic need <em>on</em> every human being” would confuse the reader (Lecture 4.6).</li>\n<li>(ii) <strong>They</strong>, a <strong>pronoun</strong> after its noun <em>saints</em>. Repeating “Saints have said” in every sentence is monotonous.</li>\n<li>(iii) <strong>As a result</strong> (or <em>Therefore</em>, <em>Thus</em>), a <strong>connector</strong> showing a result. Draft 2 of the happiness essay uses <em>As a result</em> in exactly this place.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n"});
